---
interface Props {
  lesson: "tcp" | "udp";
}

const { lesson } = Astro.props;
---

{lesson === "tcp" ? (
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Worked example · Read a TCP ACK</h3>
    <p>Suppose the first byte of a sample message is numbered <code>1</code> and the server receives five bytes in order.</p>
    <figure class="worked-diagram">
      <table>
        <caption>TCP byte numbers and the acknowledgement</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Client sends</th><th scope="col">Server replies</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody><tr><td>Bytes <code>1–5</code></td><td><code>ACK 6</code></td></tr></tbody>
      </table>
      <figcaption><code>ACK 6</code> means “I have everything through byte 5 and expect byte 6 next.” The file transfer program sends its own verdict after it checks the saved file.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
) : (
  <div class="lesson">
    <h3>Worked example · Restore the original order</h3>
    <p>Imagine the sender puts <code>A</code>, <code>B</code>, and <code>C</code> in chunks numbered <code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, and <code>3</code>. Chunk <code>2</code> is delayed.</p>
    <figure class="worked-diagram">
      <table>
        <caption>One UDP arrival order, two ways to write the file</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Arrivals</th><th scope="col">Append on arrival</th><th scope="col">Write by chunk number</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody><tr><td><code>1 → 3 → 2</code></td><td><code>ACB</code></td><td><code>ABC</code></td></tr></tbody>
      </table>
      <figcaption>Both outputs have three bytes. Only <code>ABC</code> matches the original. The application uses the chunk numbers to write each byte in its original position.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
)}
